faq:
  - q: "What makes MBA job searching different from other job searches?"
    a: "MBA recruiting operates on a compressed, structured calendar that does not exist in most job markets. On-campus recruiting for consulting and banking starts in August/September of year one — months before most MBAs feel ready. The timeline is aggressive: companies host info sessions in September, applications close in October, first rounds happen in November, and offers are extended in November/December for internships. Missing the campus recruiting window for your target function means competing against the full market later, with fewer structured pathways. This compressed calendar, combined with the salary reset and career pivot stakes, makes MBA job searching one of the highest-pressure recruiting cycles in any career stage."
  - q: "What career paths do MBA graduates typically pursue?"
    a: "The most common post-MBA career paths cluster in four areas: (1) Management Consulting — McKinsey, BCG, Bain, Deloitte, Accenture Strategy. Case interview preparation is the primary differentiator; the on-campus recruiting process is well-structured but highly competitive. (2) Investment Banking / Private Equity — associate roles at bulge brackets and elite boutiques, or PE analyst/associate roles for candidates with pre-MBA finance experience. (3) Technology / Product Management — PM and senior PM roles at Big Tech (Google, Meta, Microsoft, Amazon) and growth-stage companies. (4) General Management / Operations — rotational leadership programs at Fortune 500 companies. Each path has different recruiting timelines, preparation requirements, and compensation structures that require path-specific strategy."
  - q: "How should MBAs approach consulting recruiting?"
    a: "Consulting recruiting for MBAs is the most structured and predictable job search process in business school. MBB (McKinsey, BCG, Bain) recruit heavily at M7 programs and a short list of other target schools. Key preparation priorities: (1) Start case prep in the summer before school — candidates who begin in September are already behind. (2) Aim for 50–100 structured case practice sessions with partners, not solo reading. (3) Treat the networking phase seriously — coffees and info sessions with consultants are relationship-building, not box-checking. (4) Know which practice area and office you are targeting before applying — generic interest signals are filtered at the offer stage. (5) Prepare your 'why consulting' and 'why this firm' stories with specific, informed reasoning — MBB interviewers hear generic answers constantly."
  - q: "How should MBAs approach tech and product management recruiting?"
    a: "Tech and PM recruiting for MBAs is less structured than consulting — there is no universal on-campus cycle, timelines vary by company, and preparation requirements differ significantly. Key points: (1) Apply earlier than you think — Google, Meta, and Amazon PM pipelines fill fast and some roles close before Thanksgiving. (2) Build product fluency before recruiting — know the company's product deeply, form opinions, and be able to discuss what you would build next. (3) Your pre-MBA background matters more in tech than in consulting — MBAs with engineering, data, or product experience have a significant advantage over purely business backgrounds. (4) MBA hiring for PM roles often sits in a senior PM or PM II band — understand the leveling at your target company. (5) Salary negotiation for tech PM roles has more flexibility than consulting — total comp packages (base + bonus + equity) are highly negotiable."
  - q: "What is the biggest career coaching mistake MBAs make?"
    a: "The most common mistake MBAs make is waiting too long to start and trying to run multiple recruiting tracks simultaneously without depth in any of them. MBA programs attract ambitious people who believe they can prepare for consulting, banking, and tech recruiting at the same time — and most fail at all three by spreading preparation too thin. The second most common mistake is underinvesting in behavioral interview preparation while over-indexing on case or technical prep. The 'fit' portion of MBA recruiting at MBB, banking, and tech PM roles eliminates a surprising number of strong technical candidates who cannot tell a compelling, specific story about why they want the role, why this company, and what they will bring. Both technical and behavioral preparation must be genuinely strong."
  - q: "How do MBAs negotiate job offers?"
    a: "MBA offers — particularly at consulting and banking firms — have less flexible compensation structures than tech offers, but negotiation is still possible and expected. For consulting: base salary is typically fixed by cohort level; sign-on bonus and start date are negotiable. For banking: signing bonus is often the negotiable lever; base is relatively fixed by level. For tech: total comp (base + RSU + bonus) is the most flexible; equity package, sign-on, and even level/title can be negotiated with competing offers. The single most powerful lever across all MBA offers is a competing offer — a real alternative at a credible firm with written terms. MBAs who negotiate without a competing offer have significantly less leverage than those who have one, even if they ultimately intend to accept the original offer."
