      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">The MBA recruiting calendar — what no one tells you</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Consulting recruiting starts before school starts.</strong> MBB info sessions begin in August at M7 programs. Candidates who begin case prep in September are behind the curve. Serious consulting candidates begin preparation in June or July of the summer before school.</li>
        <li><strong>Banking recruiting is even faster.</strong> Investment banking associate applications open in August and close in October. First-round interviews happen in October/November. If you are targeting banking, your timeline starts the moment you accept your MBA offer.</li>
        <li><strong>Tech PM recruiting is less structured but not easier.</strong> Google, Meta, Amazon, and Microsoft PM recruiting happens through the fall, with some roles extending into winter. The lack of structure makes it harder to know where you stand — which is why a deliberate outreach and application strategy matters more.</li>
        <li><strong>Internship converts to full-time at high rates.</strong> At MBB and banking, 80–90% of strong summer interns receive full-time offers. This means the internship recruiting season — not the full-time recruiting season — is the critical gate. Win the internship, and the full-time offer is the likely outcome.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="content-panel">
      <h2 class="section-title section-title-glow">Path-specific preparation priorities</h2>
      <h3>Consulting (MBB / Big Four Strategy)</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>50–100 structured case practice sessions by internship application deadline</li>
        <li>3 distinct PEI stories: personal impact, entrepreneurial drive, inclusive leadership — specific and personal</li>
        <li>Clear "why consulting / why this firm / why this practice" narrative with specific knowledge of the firm's recent work</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Technology / Product Management</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Product design questions for each target company — know their products deeply, form opinions, bring specific ideas</li>
        <li>Metrics and analytical thinking: A/B testing design, success metrics, and tradeoff analysis</li>
        <li>Leadership and cross-functional influence stories — tech PM roles require demonstrated ability to ship without authority</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="margin-top:20px;">Investment Banking (Associate)</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Technical finance: DCF, LBO, accretion/dilution, M&A rationale — must be fluent, not just familiar</li>
        <li>"Why banking post-MBA" story must address the career change question credibly if you were not in banking pre-MBA</li>
        <li>Coverage group targeting: know 2–3 recent deals in your target sector and be able to discuss them in depth</li>
      </ul>
